        ],
        "abstract": "This paper presents some of the results of a theoretical examination of the effect of applying a conductive ferromagnetic coating to the conductors in a gas insulated substation (GIS) as a means of controlling disconnect transients. These transients are known to have rise times in the range 2-12ns, and to initiate a transient travelling wave overvoltage with a peak magnitude of up to 2,5pu. A broadband mathematical model has been developed in order to simulate these effects in one phase of a three phase system. Apparent permeability changes within the materials are accounted for, and the effect of field intensity upon the attenuation shown. Reductions in actual permeability due to frequency effects are discussed. Predicted attenuations introduced to a representative pulse, as well as the degradation of the 10-90% risetime of a surge voltage, are tabulated. The factor μ<inf xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">r</inf> σ is identified as both an important selection and design criterion for suitable coating materials.",
