            },
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "A J Walker",
                "firstName": "A J",
                "lastName": "Walker"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "By 1990 the total inventory of source code will be of the order of 25 000 000 000 lines. The bulk of this code is unstructured, disorganised, unmaintainable and irreplaceable. Software maintenance costs have escalated over the past decade due to increases in programmer salaries and the amount of time spent on maintenance. The cost of completely redeveloping, instead of reusing by renewing (using a superior design strategy) these existing packages is prohibitive in terms of the required human and financial resources. A technique is required to transform this unstructured software into a more maintainable form. This paper reviews the renewal of LINSIM, an analog electric network analysis and optimisation program. This package is of a sufficient size to allow worthwhile data (statistical as well as methodological) to be collected. Observations are made on the nature of the architectural, design and coding errors detected during this exercise.",
